WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.280
Captain America has appeared&nbsp;
in many movies and TV shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:07.520
but which screen adaptations wield the shield&nbsp;
with the most might? Join us as we answer that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.840
question by ranking every adaptation of the&nbsp;
original Captain America from worst to best.
 
 

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.440
1944's Captain America stands as the first&nbsp;
instance of a live-action Marvel Comics adaptation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.720
undergoing a theatrical release. It remained the&nbsp;
only one of its kind until 1977's Spider-Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.920
originally a made-for-TV movie,&nbsp;
played in international theaters.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.680
While this serial warrants attention for its&nbsp;
historic significance, it's hard to say how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.360
much it holds up on its own merits. This&nbsp;
version of Captain America isn't a World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.000
War II super soldier named Steve Rogers, for&nbsp;
one thing. Instead, he's a district attorney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.400
named Grant Gardner who uses a gun instead of&nbsp;
his signature shield. In the first episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.720
Cap investigates a series of mysterious&nbsp;
deaths linked to an ancient Mayan artifact.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.200
"I don't make excuses, but&nbsp;
I do have a definite plan."

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.520
This really sounds like a job more suited&nbsp;
for Batman or any of the other numerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.560
costumed detectives who starred&nbsp;
in adventure serials of the era.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.160
Aside from the name, costume, and a&nbsp;
vague sense that they share a powerset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.360
there's little connection between this&nbsp;
Captain America and the character in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.360
comics. But what the film lacks in fidelity&nbsp;
to its source material, it makes up for in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.440
over-the-top Hollywood fight scenes. If&nbsp;
that's your thing, it's worth a gander.
 
 
 

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.280
The mere implication that Spider-Man and&nbsp;
his Amazing Friends is anything less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.840
a groundbreaking cross-media achievement is&nbsp;
blasphemy against the nerd gods. So don't get it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.080
mixed up: We're not saying there's anything wrong&nbsp;
with Amazing Friends, only that the iteration of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.880
Steve Rogers who appears in Pawns of the Kingpin&nbsp;
is not one of Cap's more impressive turns.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.560
Unlike other Captains America, this&nbsp;
one's a glory hound who spends loads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.040
of time on athletic exhibitions to show&nbsp;
off for Empire State University students.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.640
Before one such demonstration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.287
he proudly brags about his amazing abilities&nbsp;
to a cheering crowd like a carnival barker.

00:01:43.287 --> 00:01:48.800
"Today you will see what years of&nbsp;
training and exercise have done for me!"

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.960
Not exactly humble - and then he performs feats&nbsp;
that would be utterly impossible without the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.734
super-soldier serum everyone knows gave him&nbsp;
his powers, "training and exercise" be damned.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.520
Computer animation can be one heck of a dice roll.&nbsp;
Somehow, 2014's Iron Man and Captain America:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.000
Heroes United looks like a giant cut scene&nbsp;
from an outdated video game. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.200
you could convince us 1995's&nbsp;
Toy Story came out yesterday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.760
if we didn't already know better. Something's&nbsp;
gone wrong here, but we're not sure exactly what.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.600
Instantly dated though it may be, Heroes&nbsp;
United is significant for being one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:23.600
the first contemporary instances of Cap&nbsp;
getting brainwashed into joining HYDRA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.160
This idea would prove aggressively unpopular&nbsp;
years later when replicated in Secret Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.960
a 2017 Marvel Comics crossover storyline.&nbsp;
Then again, Cap's notorious proclamation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.480
of HYDRA loyalties at Secret Empire's&nbsp;
onset paved the way for one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.560
gut-busting moments in Avengers: Endgame,&nbsp;
so we suppose it all comes out in the wash.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:42.800
"Hail Hydra."
 
 

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.560
Ultimate Avengers: The Movie and Ultimate&nbsp;
Avengers 2: Rise of The Panther predate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.040
Robert Downey Jr.'s first on-screen outing as&nbsp;
Tony Stark by roughly two years. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.800
viewers of these movies didn't necessarily&nbsp;
understand they were being prepared - or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.720
should we say indoctrinated? - to go see&nbsp;
a series of live-action Avengers movies.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.040
That said, the animated Ultimate Avengers projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:06.720
were clearly influenced by the 2002&nbsp;
comic book series The Ultimates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.534
which were tonally quite different from&nbsp;
what the live-action MCU would come to be.

00:03:10.534 --> 00:03:14.240
The Ultimates, written by Mark Millar,&nbsp;
reimagines Earth's mightiest heroes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.400
a post-9/11 setting with a grim-and-gritty&nbsp;
attitude. But Ultimate Avengers: The Movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:23.120
is a pretty cut-and-dry Avengers origin story&nbsp;
- maybe a bit too mature for the littlest kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:27.280
but a far cry from the dark territory The&nbsp;
Ultimates comics had a tendency to slide into.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.520
With their mature tone, hardly anyone at the&nbsp;
time understood why these films existed or who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:35.920
they were for, and they were quickly rendered&nbsp;
superfluous by superior Avengers cartoons.  
 
 

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.520
Is 1990's Captain America really as bad as its&nbsp;
dismal Rotten Tomatoes score makes it out to be?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.240
Is it possible that audiences&nbsp;
of the time got their hopes up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:48.720
for Marvel's answer to 1989's Batman, and&nbsp;
had their spirits crushed by a mediocre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:52.720
straight-to-VHS action romp? Of course,&nbsp;
this movie isn't particularly good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:57.079
but it's only terrible because it made us think&nbsp;
Marvel superheroes wouldn't work as movies.

00:04:01.048 --> 00:04:02.048
"Oh."

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.560
Despite all of the hokiness, this version of&nbsp;
Steve Rogers still feels relatively true to its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.920
source material. This Cap, at the very least, gets&nbsp;
his powers via a super soldier program in WWII,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.720
gets frozen, and ends up having to awkwardly&nbsp;
adjust to modernity once he unfreezes. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:22.080
it's not all true to the comics: Red Skull is&nbsp;
inexplicably Italian, and Cap's mask includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.600
deeply creepy rubber ears, presumably as a means&nbsp;
to strike additional terror into his adversaries.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.040
We don't want to sound overly dismissive&nbsp;
of an Avengers adaptation that isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:35.680
targeted at an English-speaking audience. This&nbsp;
adaptation may very well fly over our heads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:39.600
only to land gracefully and with precision&nbsp;
for the Japanese kids it's meant for. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.000
if we understand correctly, Disk Wars is basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.920
a combination of Avengers and Pokémon, in&nbsp;
which Cap and his teammates are trapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.640
in pocket-sized plastic disks and must be&nbsp;
summoned when a combat-situation arises.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.880
It's not an ideal concept for Cap, however,&nbsp;
who's a little too much of an anachronism to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.680
fit into a Pokémon-style world. Even when the&nbsp;
storyline continues in Marvel Future Avengers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:02.240
an anime series that drops&nbsp;
the shtick with the disks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.440
Cap doesn't necessarily make a&nbsp;
case for himself as an anime star.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.320
In 1979's made-for-TV movies Captain&nbsp;
America and Captain America II:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:14.160
Death Too Soon, Steve Rogers is a&nbsp;
monumentally chillaxed and creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.800
dude who's never seen an Arctic ice field in&nbsp;
his life. His father served in World War II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.320
which means by the time Steve was born,&nbsp;
Hitler was already too dead to punch.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.560
Late '70s Steve tosses around a shield, but&nbsp;
he also cruises in a customized red, white,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.720
and blue motorcycle that converts into a hang&nbsp;
glider for the frequent occasions in which he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.400
finds himself needing a hang glider.&nbsp;
This Cap's also got a tricked-out van,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.901
as well as a bulky blue and white helmet&nbsp;
to keep his head safe on the road.

00:05:37.901 --> 00:05:41.360
This Cap strays too far from the original&nbsp;
for us to really consider him an authentic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.880
Captain America. But even if he's&nbsp;
not at all a comics-accurate Cap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.320
he still seems kind of awesome.  
 
 

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.000
The '90s was a pretty wild time for Marvel&nbsp;
animation. X-Men kicked off in 1992,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.560
setting the template for that era's batch of&nbsp;
shows. Spider-Man, Fantastic Four, Iron Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.960
The Incredible Hulk, and Avengers: United They&nbsp;
Stand all went on to varying degrees of success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:05.760
Their interconnected continuity isn't as overt,&nbsp;
or as important, as it is in the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:09.600
Universe, or even as it typically is in the&nbsp;
comics. But nevertheless, it's certainly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.760
most ambitious shared universe Saturday morning&nbsp;
cartoons could possibly muster at the time.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:17.760
Captain America teams up with Wolverine&nbsp;
in a World War II flashback in X-Men's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.040
"Old Soldiers." He also makes&nbsp;
multiple appearances in Spider-Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.840
most prominently during the Secret Wars crossover,&nbsp;
which also includes the Fantastic Four, Iron Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:29.680
and the Black Cat. Perhaps most importantly,&nbsp;
this Captain America marks the millennial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:35.201
generation's first exposure to a solidly-executed,&nbsp;
almost-comics-accurate version of the character.

00:06:35.201 --> 00:06:39.680
This rendering of Marvel's signature team&nbsp;
remained on the air from 2013 to 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.840
longer than any other Avengers show, and it&nbsp;
just so happens to coincide with the team's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.000
all-time highest level of cultural visibility.&nbsp;
So, if we're ranking these Captains America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.680
partially on their scope of influence,&nbsp;
even a casual and infrequent Avengers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:55.040
Assemble viewer would have to place its&nbsp;
Cap among the mightiest of this lot.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:59.840
During the '90s explosion of Marvel animated&nbsp;
projects aiming to capitalize on X-Men's success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:02.240
Cap never got his own show. That means Assemble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:05.520
with about half a decade on the air,&nbsp;
contains a larger total quantity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.760
televised Captain America than you'll find in&nbsp;
any other existing animated Marvel timeline.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:13.760
But, if we may pick a nit, a viewer might&nbsp;
notice that the Roger Craig Smith-voiced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:17.600
take on Steve Rogers comes close to&nbsp;
flat-out mimicking Chris Evans' MCU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:21.520
performance. So while we've got to dock this&nbsp;
Cap some points for a lack of creativity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:25.040
perhaps it earns those points back for&nbsp;
its strength of cross-media synergy.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:29.200
"Earth is under imminent cosmic threat.&nbsp;
It's unlike anything we've ever faced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:31.360
and Skull has information on how to stop it."

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:36.400
Today, The Marvel Super Heroes is better&nbsp;
understood as pop art, rather than action-centric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:40.400
television. You've only got to pull Cap's&nbsp;
adventures barely out of context to recreate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:46.000
them as surreal, mind-expanding fever dreams.&nbsp;
But the Andy Warhol-esque elements of 1966's Cap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.240
aren't the sole reason for his high status on&nbsp;
this list. Each of the show's heroes boasts his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:54.720
own theme song penned by composer Jack Urbont,&nbsp;
and Cap's musical call-to-attention is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.720
only jingle from that generation of Marvel&nbsp;
cartoons many people still remember today.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:07:59.600
The lyrics trumpet,

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.840
"When Captain America throws his mighty shield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:04.640
all those who choose to&nbsp;
oppose his shield must yield!"

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:08.960
In terms of classic superhero songs, that&nbsp;
one's almost up there with Batman's iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:14.000
"Na na na na" from the 60s show, and that tune&nbsp;
about Spider-Man doing whatever a spider can.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.160
It's iconic as all get-out, and pairs beautifully&nbsp;
with this cartoon's out-there aspects.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.480
After Iron Man hit theaters and revealed&nbsp;
Marvel Studios' intention to assemble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.000
the Avengers on screen, plenty of people&nbsp;
weren't sure exactly how that would look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:30.160
This strange and ambiguous period in&nbsp;
Marvel-licensed media resulted in The Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.800
Earth's Mightiest Heroes, which originally&nbsp;
aired on Disney XD in the fall of 2010.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:39.200
The series gives a glimpse of how the MCU might&nbsp;
have panned out if Hank Pym and Janet Van Dyne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:43.040
co-founded the Avengers. Ever wanted to know what&nbsp;
it would have looked like if Black Widow eschewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:47.120
Avengers membership and stuck to professional&nbsp;
espionage? How about if Black Panther joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:51.200
the team in a more official capacity, and much&nbsp;
earlier? What might've happened if the secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:55.920
Skrull invasion took place before Iron Man and&nbsp;
Cap left the franchise? The answers to these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.800
questions, and many more, can all be found within&nbsp;
Earth's Mightiest Heroes. No wonder fans adore it.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.560
Earth's Mightiest Heroes' Captain America&nbsp;
carries himself a bit more obviously like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:08.000
an old man in a younger man's body&nbsp;
than other entries on this list. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.680
also pushes back against Iron Man's&nbsp;
confidence in the value of futurism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:15.760
and gives him grief about having no idea what&nbsp;
to do in a fist fight. He's multifaceted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:19.501
entertaining, and an essential part of&nbsp;
the best Avengers cartoon ever made.
 
 
 &nbsp;

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:23.920
By the time Captain America: The First Avenger&nbsp;
arrived, we had seen one or two reverent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:28.400
well-executed animated incarnations of Steve&nbsp;
Rogers. But prior attempts to transfer the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:32.880
star-spangled soldier to live-action hadn't&nbsp;
panned out especially well. For whatever reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:36.640
creatives weren't able to quite effectively&nbsp;
translate a superhero with such an optimistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:41.440
disposition to a live-action real world that can&nbsp;
often feel dark and dreary. That all changed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:45.840
the MCU, and no matter what the future holds for&nbsp;
Captain America and other comic book characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:49.280
on screen, fans will be able to point to&nbsp;
The First Avenger and give it credit for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:53.680
breaking that cycle. In the wake of that movie's&nbsp;
release, Cap swiftly became one of the MCU's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.000
popular characters - and a lot of that has to&nbsp;
do with Chris Evans' pitch-perfect performance.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.520
In his take on the role, Chris Evans&nbsp;
emphasizes the "kid from Brooklyn" aspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:06.320
of Cap over his super soldier side. His temporal&nbsp;
displacement is often played up for corny humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:09.840
rather than as an ever-present source of&nbsp;
angst or resentment - which also means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.360
that when his man-out-of-time nature is&nbsp;
taken seriously, it hits all the harder.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:19.280
"It's been so long… so long."

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:22.640
"Well I couldn't leave my best girl."

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.200
Moreover, despite being a century-old&nbsp;
scientifically-enhanced super human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:31.360
Cap adds a much-needed sense of down-to-Earth&nbsp;
relatability to an ensemble of billionaires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:35.040
gods, robots, and monsters. It's hard&nbsp;
to imagine that there will ever be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:38.400
live-action version of Captain America&nbsp;
that ever does better than this one.

